export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"bimcraft","addon":"dome","name":"Dome generator","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"dome-start","scope":"addon:dome","title":"Dome generator: getting started","summary":"Make a thin concrete shell dome from its diameter and height: a spherical or elliptical shell sprayed inside an inflated form over foam, on a stem wall and ring beam, with framed openings, quantities, forces under weight, snow, wind and earthquake, bending, a buckling figure and an IFC file. Metric or feet and inches. A drawing aid, not a design.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["dome","wind","earthquake","seismic","snow","bending","buckling","load factors","feet","inches","imperial","rebar","bars","concrete shell","thin shell","monolithic dome","sprayed concrete","shotcrete","inflated form","foam","ellipsoid","sphere","hemisphere","stem wall","ring beam","membrane forces","ring tension","IFC","quantities","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"what-the-dimensions-mean","text":"What the dimensions mean","level":2},{"id":"where-the-figures-come-from","text":"Where the figures come from","level":2},{"id":"specs-are-side-add-ons","text":"Specs are side add-ons","level":2},{"id":"units","text":"Units","level":2},{"id":"openings-with-frames-and-bars","text":"Openings with frames, and bars","level":2},{"id":"the-forces","text":"The forces","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do","text":"What it does not do","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESwitch the add-on on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then open the \u003Cstrong\u003EDome generator\u003C/strong\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E, choose the curve (part of a sphere, or half an ellipse), and type the diameter, the height of the curve and, if you want the dome to start above the ground, a stem wall height. All in millimetres.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings\u003C/strong\u003E, set each door or window: where it stands round the dome (an angle), its width, height and sill.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cstrong\u003EResult\u003C/strong\u003E: the plan and the section, the quantities (form surface, foam, sprayed concrete, ring beam, floor slab, an estimate of the steel), the clear inside (floor area, usable floor with headroom, height, volume) and the forces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ESave as an IFC file\u003C/strong\u003E for the 3D model, or \u003Cstrong\u003ESave the report (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E for the figures.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-the-dimensions-mean\"\u003EWhat the dimensions mean\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe diameter and the height are those of the \u003Cstrong\u003Einflated form\u003C/strong\u003E, which is the outside of the foam. The foam is sprayed on the inside of the form and the concrete on the inside of the foam, so the room inside is smaller than the form. The result gives the clear diameter, height, floor area and volume.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPart of a sphere\u003C/strong\u003E: at most half a sphere. A height below half the diameter gives a low dome that pushes outward on its ring.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHalf an ellipse\u003C/strong\u003E: flatter than a half sphere, or taller.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStem wall\u003C/strong\u003E: a straight wall under the curve, in the same layers.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe shell is thinnest at the crown. \u003Cstrong\u003EExtra thickness at the base\u003C/strong\u003E makes it grow evenly toward the bottom.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-figures-come-from\"\u003EWhere the figures come from\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EShell thickness, cover, concrete strength, the steel ratio and the load factors are set by norms. The generator holds \u003Cstrong\u003Eno norm values\u003C/strong\u003E. Leave those boxes empty and each one is filled:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efrom the \u003Cstrong\u003Espec\u003C/strong\u003E you picked, when a spec add-on is switched on and has a value for it, or\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Efrom a \u003Cstrong\u003Estarting figure\u003C/strong\u003E, which is a round number so that something is drawn. A starting figure is not from any standard, and the box says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType your own figure in a box to overrule either. The result then says when your figure breaks the spec.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"specs-are-side-add-ons\"\u003ESpecs are side add-ons\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAdd \u003Cstrong\u003EDome spec: United States\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EDome spec: Europe\u003C/strong\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E, then pick it under \u003Cstrong\u003ESpec\u003C/strong\u003E. The table under the picker lists every rule in the spec with its standard, its clause and how we read it: in the standard itself, in a document that quotes the standard, or not at all. Many rules are sentences, not numbers (buckling, how a shell may be analysed, wind and snow on domes, sprayed concrete testing, foam and fire, frost): they are there for you and your engineer to read, and the ones that most need a decision come up as notes under Checks. Check each value against the current edition and your local amendments before you rely on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EShape\u003C/strong\u003E, \u003Cstrong\u003EUnits\u003C/strong\u003E switches every box and every result between millimetres and kilonewtons, and feet, inches and pounds. The dome does not change: only how it is shown.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"openings-with-frames-and-bars\"\u003EOpenings with frames, and bars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003EOpenings\u003C/strong\u003E, a frame width and a frame extra thickness put a band of thicker concrete round every opening: at each side, over the head and under the sill. Under \u003Cstrong\u003EConcrete shell\u003C/strong\u003E, a bar size and a spacing count one mat of bars at that spacing both ways over the shell, and the result says what steel ratio that gives at the crown and at the base.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-forces\"\u003EThe forces\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cstrong\u003ELoads for the first forces\u003C/strong\u003E you set the snow load, a wind pressure, an earthquake force as a share of the weight, the concrete&#039;s stiffness, how the base is held, and a steel stress. Under \u003Cstrong\u003ELoad factors\u003C/strong\u003E, the factors are 1 until a spec fills them from its code or you type your own; the result always says whether the forces are factored. The result then gives:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMembrane forces\u003C/strong\u003E for own weight and for own weight with snow: the force along the curve and round the dome at nine heights, the largest compression, where tension round the dome begins, the outward push and ring tension at the springline, and the pressure under the ring beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWind and earthquake\u003C/strong\u003E: the forces each adds along the curve, round the dome and in shear, the sideways force and overturning at the floor, and whether one side of the base lifts when own weight is at its least.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBending\u003C/strong\u003E: the shell solved with its bending for the loads that are the same all the way round. This is where the moment at the ring beam, at the top of a stem wall and where the thickness changes comes from, with the tension in the ring beam, the largest stresses and how far the crown drops. Choose whether the base is fixed to the ring beam, held but free to turn, or free to spread against the ring beam.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBuckling\u003C/strong\u003E: the pressure a perfect shell of this shape would buckle at, against the load on it. Real concrete shells buckle far below that; type your engineer&#039;s reduction to see the margin that is left.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EForces the openings interrupt\u003C/strong\u003E: what each opening cuts, for its frame and steel to carry round.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf you type a steel stress, each tension is also given as force divided by that stress, as a steel area, and nothing more.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do\"\u003EWhat it does not do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt does not design the dome. The forces are linear elastic theory for uncracked concrete under simple load patterns. The wind is a textbook pattern, not your code&#039;s coefficients; the earthquake is a static sideways force. Bending under wind and earthquake, uneven snow, temperature, shrinkage, cracking and creep are not calculated, and the bending round an opening is not either. The bar count has no laps, no ring beam cage and no bars round openings. Nothing is checked against your site, your soil or your local code. A licensed engineer must design and check the dome.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons, then open the Dome generator tab. Under Shape, choose the curve (part of a sphere, or half an ellipse), and type the diameter, the height of the curve and, if you want the dome to start above the ground, a stem wall height. All in millimetres. Under Openings, set each door or window: where it stands round the dome (an angle), its width, height and sill. Read the Result: the plan and the section, the quantities (form surface, foam, sprayed concrete, ring beam, floor slab, an estimate of the steel), the clear inside (floor area, usable floor with headroom, height, volume) and the forces. Press Save as an IFC file for the 3D model, or Save the report (CSV) for the figures. What the dimensions mean The diameter and the height are those of the inflated form, which is the outside of the foam. The foam is sprayed on the inside of the form and the concrete on the inside of the foam, so the room inside is smaller than the form. The result gives the clear diameter, height, floor area and volume. Part of a sphere: at most half a sphere. A height below half the diameter gives a low dome that pushes outward on its ring. Half an ellipse: flatter than a half sphere, or taller. Stem wall: a straight wall under the curve, in the same layers. The shell is thinnest at the crown. Extra thickness at the base makes it grow evenly toward the bottom. Where the figures come from Shell thickness, cover, concrete strength, the steel ratio and the load factors are set by norms. The generator holds no norm values. Leave those boxes empty and each one is filled: from the spec you picked, when a spec add-on is switched on and has a value for it, or from a starting figure, which is a round number so that something is drawn. A starting figure is not from any standard, and the box says so. Type your own figure in a box to overrule either. The result then says when your figure breaks the spec. Specs are side add-ons Add Dome spec: United States or Dome spec: Europe in Settings › Add-ons, then pick it under Spec. The table under the picker lists every rule in the spec with its standard, its clause and how we read it: in the standard itself, in a document that quotes the standard, or not at all. Many rules are sentences, not numbers (buckling, how a shell may be analysed, wind and snow on domes, sprayed concrete testing, foam and fire, frost): they are there for you and your engineer to read, and the ones that most need a decision come up as notes under Checks. Check each value against the current edition and your local amendments before you rely on it. Units Under Shape, Units switches every box and every result between millimetres and kilonewtons, and feet, inches and pounds. The dome does not change: only how it is shown. Openings with frames, and bars Under Openings, a frame width and a frame extra thickness put a band of thicker concrete round every opening: at each side, over the head and under the sill. Under Concrete shell, a bar size and a spacing count one mat of bars at that spacing both ways over the shell, and the result says what steel ratio that gives at the crown and at the base. The forces Under Loads for the first forces you set the snow load, a wind pressure, an earthquake force as a share of the weight, the concrete's stiffness, how the base is held, and a steel stress. Under Load factors, the factors are 1 until a spec fills them from its code or you type your own; the result always says whether the forces are factored. The result then gives: Membrane forces for own weight and for own weight with snow: the force along the curve and round the dome at nine heights, the largest compression, where tension round the dome begins, the outward push and ring tension at the springline, and the pressure under the ring beam. Wind and earthquake: the forces each adds along the curve, round the dome and in shear, the sideways force and overturning at the floor, and whether one side of the base lifts when own weight is at its least. Bending: the shell solved with its bending for the loads that are the same all the way round. This is where the moment at the ring beam, at the top of a stem wall and where the thickness changes comes from, with the tension in the ring beam, the largest stresses and how far the crown drops. Choose whether the base is fixed to the ring beam, held but free to turn, or free to spread against the ring beam. Buckling: the pressure a perfect shell of this shape would buckle at, against the load on it. Real concrete shells buckle far below that; type your engineer's reduction to see the margin that is left. Forces the openings interrupt: what each opening cuts, for its frame and steel to carry round. If you type a steel stress, each tension is also given as force divided by that stress, as a steel area, and nothing more. What it does not do It does not design the dome. The forces are linear elastic theory for uncracked concrete under simple load patterns. The wind is a textbook pattern, not your code's coefficients; the earthquake is a static sideways force. Bending under wind and earthquake, uneven snow, temperature, shrinkage, cracking and creep are not calculated, and the bending round an opening is not either. The bar count has no laps, no ring beam cage and no bars round openings. Nothing is checked against your site, your soil or your local code. A licensed engineer must design and check the dome."},{"slug":"dome-bimcraft","scope":"addon:dome","title":"Dome generator: the dome in your model or drawing","summary":"Place the dome in a BimCraft model as elements with properties and quantities, or draw its plan and section in CadCraft. Both save an IFC file from the panel.","section":"tasks","order":2,"keywords":["dome","place","IFC","elements","roof","footing","slab","wall","covering","revolved solid","plan","section","layers","DOME","undo"],"headings":[{"id":"in-bimcraft","text":"In BimCraft","level":2},{"id":"in-cadcraft","text":"In CadCraft","level":2},{"id":"the-ifc-file","text":"The IFC file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"in-bimcraft\"\u003EIn BimCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EPlace in the model\u003C/strong\u003E. The dome is written on the model&#039;s first level as elements: the ring beam, the floor slab, the stem wall, the shell, the foam and the form membrane. Each is a true solid of revolution with a \u003Ccode\u003ECadCraft_Dome\u003C/code\u003E property set and base quantities, so it schedules and saves in the IFC file like anything else. The dome&#039;s floor stands at the level&#039;s height.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIt is one step: \u003Cstrong\u003EUndo\u003C/strong\u003E takes the whole dome back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlacing again replaces the dome \u003Cstrong\u003Eof the same name\u003C/strong\u003E. Give a second dome another name.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPosition\u003C/strong\u003E moves the dome&#039;s centre from the level&#039;s origin.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERemove the dome\u003C/strong\u003E takes the elements of that name out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe openings are real gaps in the shell, the foam and the membrane. No door or window is put in them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"in-cadcraft\"\u003EIn CadCraft\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EDraw plan and section\u003C/strong\u003E. The plan and the section are drawn as circles, arcs, polylines, text and dimensions on \u003Ccode\u003EDOME-\u003C/code\u003E layers, in the drawing&#039;s own units. They are ordinary entities: they plot and export to DXF and DWG, and they do not follow later changes to the dome. Press \u003Cstrong\u003ERemove the dome drawing\u003C/strong\u003E, change the dome, and draw again.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-ifc-file\"\u003EThe IFC file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESave as an IFC file\u003C/strong\u003E writes an IFC4 file of the same dome from either app: the ring beam as a footing, the slab, the stem wall, the shell as a dome roof, the foam and membrane as coverings, with colours, properties, quantities and materials. The design figures and the first membrane forces are written on the building. Open it in BimCraft or any IFC viewer. Reinforcement is not in the file.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"In BimCraft Press Place in the model. The dome is written on the model's first level as elements: the ring beam, the floor slab, the stem wall, the shell, the foam and the form membrane. Each is a true solid of revolution with a CadCraft_Dome property set and base quantities, so it schedules and saves in the IFC file like anything else. The dome's floor stands at the level's height. It is one step: Undo takes the whole dome back. Placing again replaces the dome of the same name. Give a second dome another name. Position moves the dome's centre from the level's origin. Remove the dome takes the elements of that name out. The openings are real gaps in the shell, the foam and the membrane. No door or window is put in them. In CadCraft Press Draw plan and section. The plan and the section are drawn as circles, arcs, polylines, text and dimensions on DOME- layers, in the drawing's own units. They are ordinary entities: they plot and export to DXF and DWG, and they do not follow later changes to the dome. Press Remove the dome drawing, change the dome, and draw again. The IFC file Save as an IFC file writes an IFC4 file of the same dome from either app: the ring beam as a footing, the slab, the stem wall, the shell as a dome roof, the foam and membrane as coverings, with colours, properties, quantities and materials. The design figures and the first membrane forces are written on the building. Open it in BimCraft or any IFC viewer. Reinforcement is not in the file."}]};
